# input/world/first-room.shape
#
# A TEST FIXTURE, not a world. The real map format is the game's own — see
# docs/datapath-the-world-of-shapes.md — and nothing here is ever turned into
# server-side collision, height, or navigation data.
#
# This file exists for two jobs. It gives the renderer something to draw before
# the extraction pipeline is standing, so a window with a triangle in it does
# not have to wait on a toolchain. And it is small enough to reason about by
# hand, which is what you want the day the camera matrix is wrong and you need a
# scene where you already know every coordinate.
#
# The smallest useful thing: a floor big enough to walk across, four walls so
# there is an inside, one ramp so height exists, and two blocks to stand behind.
#
# ─── format ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   quad  x,y,z  x,y,z  x,y,z  x,y,z   c=N     four corners, wound counter-clockwise
#   tri   x,y,z  x,y,z  x,y,z          c=N     three corners, same winding
#   box   x,y,z → x,y,z                c=N     shorthand: expands to six quads
#
# Coordinates are the server's own world units, so nothing needs transforming
# between what we draw and what it thinks. c= is a PALETTE SLOT, never a colour
# — which is what lets one file render four different ways. Slot meanings are in
# docs/datapath-the-world-of-shapes.md; the values are in the scheme tables.
#
# Winding decides which way a face points, and facing decides its shade, since
# there is no lighting at runtime. A quad wound the wrong way is a face that is
# both dark and invisible from the side you meant.
#
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# the ground — 64 units square, slot 1
quad    0,0,0     64,0,0    64,64,0    0,64,0     c=1

# four walls, 8 high, slot 2. Note the winding: each is wound so its face turns
# INWARD, because the room is meant to be seen from inside.
quad    0,0,0     0,0,8     64,0,8     64,0,0     c=2
quad   64,0,0    64,0,8    64,64,8    64,64,0     c=2
quad   64,64,0   64,64,8    0,64,8     0,64,0     c=2
quad    0,64,0    0,64,8     0,0,8      0,0,0     c=2

# a ramp against the north wall — this is how height happens without curves.
# Two triangles and a quad: the sloped face, and the two sides that close it.
tri    16,64,0   32,64,0   32,56,0                c=3
quad   16,64,0   32,64,0   32,56,4   16,56,4      c=3
tri    16,64,0   16,56,4   16,56,0                c=3
tri    32,64,0   32,56,0   32,56,4                c=3

# two blocks to stand behind. The whole point of these is collision: they are
# the first test of whether "can I see you" and "can I walk here" agree with
# what is on screen.
box    20,20,0 → 28,28,6                          c=4
box    40,36,0 → 44,52,10                         c=4

# a low step, one unit high, to find out whether small height changes read at
# all in flat shading — or whether they need an edge line to be visible.
box     8,8,0  → 14,14,1                          c=3
